{"tool_name":"bioconductor-multihiccompare","contents":["bioconda","bioconductor","biocontainers","bioschemas","biotools","oeb"],"identifiers":["biotools:bioconductor-multihiccompare","bioconductor:multihiccompare","bioconda:bioconductor-multihiccompare"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"bioconductor-multihiccompare","identifiers":["doi:10.18129/b9.bioc.multihiccompare"],"license":"MIT","summary":"Normalize and detect differences between hi-c datasets when replicates of each experimental condition are available","home_url":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.11/bioc/html/multiHiCcompare.html","keywords":[],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":3},"biotools":{"id":"bioconductor-multihiccompare","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/multiHiCcompare","license":"MIT","summary":"multiHiCcompare provides functions for joint normalization and difference detection in multiple Hi-C datasets. This extension of the original HiCcompare package now allows for Hi-C experiments with more than 2 groups and multiple samples per group. multiHiCcompare operates on processed Hi-C data in the form of sparse upper triangular matrices. It accepts four column (chromosome, region1, region2, IF) tab-separated text files storing chromatin interaction matrices. multiHiCcompare provides cyclic loess and fast loess (fastlo) methods adapted to jointly normalizing Hi-C data. Additionally, it provides a general linear model (GLM) framework adapting the edgeR package to detect differences in Hi-C data in a distance dependent manner.","addition_date":"2026-08-30T02:47:19.401597Z","last_update_date":"2026-08-30T02:47:19.403356Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"version":["1.30.0"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:bioconductor-multihiccompare","credit":[{"name":"John Stansfield","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]},{"name":"Mikhail Dozmorov","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer","Maintainer"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/multiHiCcompare"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"name":"multiHiCcompare","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"gh2biotools","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btz048"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"multiHiCcompare","home":"https://bio.tools/bioconductor-multihiccompare","license":"MIT","version":"1.30.0","summary":"multiHiCcompare provides functions for joint normalization and difference detection in multiple Hi-C datasets. This extension of the original HiCcompare package now allows for Hi-C experiments with more than 2 groups and multiple samples per group. multiHiCcompare operates on processed Hi-C data in the form of sparse upper triangular matrices. It accepts four column (chromosome, region1, region2, IF) tab-separated text files storing chromatin interaction matrices. multiHiCcompare provides cyclic loess and fast loess (fastlo) methods adapted to jointly normalizing Hi-C data. Additionally, it provides a general linear model (GLM) framework adapting the edgeR package to detect differences in Hi-C data in a distance dependent manner.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"bioconductor":{"name":"multiHiCcompare","version":"1.30.0","license":"MIT + file LICENSE","summary":"Normalize and detect differences between Hi-C datasets when replicates of each experimental condition are available","description":"multiHiCcompare provides functions for joint normalization and difference detection in multiple Hi-C datasets. This extension of the original HiCcompare package now allows for Hi-C experiments with more than 2 groups and multiple samples per group. multiHiCcompare operates on processed Hi-C data in the form of sparse upper triangular matrices. It accepts four column (chromosome, region1, region2, IF) tab-separated text files storing chromatin interaction matrices. multiHiCcompare provides cyclic loess and fast loess (fastlo) methods adapted to jointly normalizing Hi-C data. Additionally, it